{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"signature-drink-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Signature drink development track","description":"Develops a house signature drink in gated steps - brief, base recipe, taste and balance, an alcohol-free twin, then a batch plan and spec card - for a wedding, party or home bar.","category":"drinks","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["design","build","verify"],"role":["home-cook","individual"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","preferences"],"output":["plan","table","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["signature-drink","recipe-development","drink-balance","alcohol-free","responsible-drinking"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["fix-unbalanced-cocktail","invent-alcohol-free-cocktail","design-cocktail-menu","make-cordial-or-shrub"],"personas":["mixology-coach"]},"args":[{"name":"occasion","description":"What the drink is for (a wedding, a birthday, a house drink for the home bar), the number of guests, the season, and any theme, colours or story you want it to tell.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"tastes_and_limits","description":"Flavours and spirits you love or avoid, whether it should be alcohol-free only, budget, equipment, how much prep time you have, and any allergies.","type":"text","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"brief","file":"steps/01-brief.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve"},{"id":"base-recipe","file":"steps/02-base-recipe.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"taste-and-balance","file":"steps/03-taste-and-balance.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve"},{"id":"alcohol-free-twin","file":"steps/04-alcohol-free-twin.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"batch-and-spec","file":"steps/05-batch-and-spec.md","stage":"build","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Develops one signature drink the way bar teams do: agree a brief, build from a proven template, test and adjust one change at a time from the user's real tasting notes, make an alcohol-free twin of equal quality, then write a batch plan and a spec card anyone can follow. Each step produces one artefact and stops for approval; later steps build on approved versions. The drink stays moderate in strength, never invents tasting results the user has not reported, and treats the alcohol-free version as a first-class drink. If the occasion is alcohol-free only, step 4 becomes the main build and the base recipe is alcohol-free from the start. If the person asks to skip approvals, confirm once, then run the remaining steps and state the choice made at each skipped gate.","steps":[{"id":"brief","file":"steps/01-brief.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Brief\n\nAgree what the drink must be before writing a recipe.\n\nOccasion: {{occasion}}\nTastes and limits: {{tastes_and_limits}}\n\n1. If the guest count, season, flavour likes or equipment are missing, ask for them in one message and stop.\n2. Write a brief: the feeling and story of the drink, flavour direction (for example bright and citrusy, bitter and aromatic, rich and spiced), serve (long or short, glass, ice), target strength (moderate), and practical limits (prep time, equipment, budget, allergens).\n3. Offer three short concepts that fit the brief, each with its template family (sour, highball, spritz, stirred, punch), main flavours and why it suits the occasion.\n4. Note who needs the alcohol-free version (drivers, pregnant guests, under-age guests, non-drinkers) so it is planned from the start.\n\nStop for approval of one concept."},{"id":"base-recipe","file":"steps/02-base-recipe.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Base recipe\n\nWrite a first recipe for the approved concept.\n\n1. Start from the template's proven ratio (for example a sour around 2:1:1 spirit, citrus, sugar, or a spritz built on the sparkling element) and say which ratio you used.\n2. Give the recipe in ml for one drink, with method (shake, stir or build), dilution, glass, ice and garnish.\n3. Give recipes for any homemade syrups or infusions it needs, with fridge storage and a conservative keeping time.\n4. Give the user a tasting plan: make it, taste for sweet, sour, bitter, strength and dilution, and write down what they notice. Offer a short note template.\n\nStop and wait for the user's tasting notes. Do not assume how it tasted."},{"id":"taste-and-balance","file":"steps/03-taste-and-balance.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Taste and balance\n\nAdjust the recipe from the user's tasting notes only.\n\n1. If no tasting notes have been given, ask for them and stop.\n2. Read the notes and name the main imbalance (too sweet, too sour, too strong, thin, flat aroma, too diluted).\n3. Change one thing at a time, in small amounts (for example 5 ml of syrup or citrus), and say what to expect.\n4. Give the revised recipe and a short table of versions so far: version, change, result.\n5. Repeat with each new set of notes until the user says it is right. Keep the strength moderate; if a change pushes it stronger, offer a longer serve instead.\n\nStop for approval of the final recipe."},{"id":"alcohol-free-twin","file":"steps/04-alcohol-free-twin.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Alcohol-free twin\n\nBuild an alcohol-free version as good as the original, not juice in the same glass.\n\n1. List what the alcohol was doing in the drink (body, warmth, bitterness, aroma, dryness) and how to replace each: tea, verjus, shrubs, bitter or spiced syrups, saline, tonic, or alcohol-free spirits if the user wants them.\n2. Write the twin recipe in ml with method, glass and a garnish that matches the original so it feels part of the same menu.\n3. Note that some alcohol-free spirits, bitters and kombucha can contain traces of alcohol, and offer a fully 0.0 option for anyone who must avoid alcohol completely.\n4. Give a tasting plan as in step 2, then adjust from the user's notes one change at a time.\n\nStop for approval of the twin."},{"id":"batch-and-spec","file":"steps/05-batch-and-spec.md","stage":"build","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Batch plan and spec card\n\nTurn both approved recipes into something that works on the day.\n\n1. Quantities: servings per guest for the occasion, split between the two versions, and batch quantities including the water normally added by shaking or stirring, with ice needs.\n2. Prep timeline: what to make days ahead (syrups), the day before (batches, chilled), and on the day (citrus, garnish), with fridge space.\n3. Spec cards: one card per drink with name, recipe for one and for the batch, glass, ice, garnish, method and a one-line description for a menu sign.\n4. Hosting notes: label the two drinks clearly, keep water and food out, modest serve sizes, no drinking games, nobody serving alcohol to under-age or visibly drunk guests, and transport home planned. Say that standard drink definitions differ by country.\n\nThis is the last step."}]}
